- 11 heptominoes (7 cells each) = 77 cells total
- Arranged into a 7×11 rectangle
- The result should be valid math equations reading across
- The example alternative fact given is ""24 + 25 ≠ 2 + 3""

**Key clues from the flavor text:**
- ""space alien from the fifth dimension"" - possible reference to 5th row/column
- ""four or five math lies"" - some equations will still be wrong (marked/indicated)
- ""math is like two perpendicular lines..."" - this trailing ellipsis strongly signals the punchline is the answer

**The joke structure:**
The setup ""math is like two perpendicular lines..."" is a classic math joke. The punchline plays on the fact that perpendicular lines meet at exactly one point — so math ""has a point.""

**Solution approach:**
After arranging the 11 heptominoes into the 7×11 grid so that each row forms a valid math equation (fixing the alternative facts like 24 + 25 ≠ 2 + 3), the remaining ""confused gibberish"" cells or the marked positions of the corrected math lies would spell out the punchline to the joke.

The joke completes as: ""Math is like two perpendicular lines... it has a point.""

